==English==

===Etymology===
{{lbor|en|ang|hlāford}}. {{doublet|en|lord|laird}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# An [[Anglo-Saxon]] [[lord]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:John Allen (historian)|John Allen]]|chapter=Tardy Growth of Many of the Royal Prerogatives|title=Inquiry into the Rise and Growth of the Royal Prerogative in England|location=London|publisher={{...|Published by}}[[w:Longman|Longman, Rees, Orme, Brown, and Green]],{{nb...|Paternoster-Row}}|year=1830|page=44|passage=The King was considered as the '''hlaford''' of the nation; and in consequence of that supposition, the security given to inferior '''hlafords''' against their particular retainers was in his case extended to all his subjects.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=William C. Pearce; Samuel Hague|chapter=Saxon and Danish Period|title=Analysis of English History. A Text-Book for Colleges and Schools.|location=London|publisher=Thos. Murby,{{nb...|32, Bouverie Street, Fleet Street}}|year=1866|pages=18–19|passage=The Eorls constituted the nobility of the land, and were subdivided into two orders, the {{smc|'''Hlafords'''}} or land-owners, and the {{smc|Sithcundmen}}, who were nobles by birth, but less wealthy than the '''hlafords'''. To this class, too, belonged the {{smc|Thanes}}, who originally were those to whom land was granted, as a feudal tenure; this title, however, in due time became equivalent to Earl, the ''King’s thane'' ranking with the '''hlafords''', and the ''lesser thane'' with the sithcundmen.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Paul Vinogradoff|title=The Growth of the Manor|year=1905|page=240|passage=In the light of these ecclesiastical donations, the assignment of hides to secular thanes mentioned in the enactments of Ine almost looks like the institution of '''hlafords''' over districts rated at a certain number of hides: these '''hlafords''' were answerable for a certain proportion of actual settlers on the land they had received, and there is nothing to show that only new colonists were meant:{{...}}}}

{{C|en|Nobility}}

==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ang|laford||Mercian}}
* {{alter|ang|hlaferd}}

===Etymology===
From earlier {{m|ang|hlāfweard}}, from {{m|ang|hlāf||bread}} + {{m|ang|weard||ward}}. See also {{m|ang|hlǣfdīġe}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|hlāford}}

===Noun===
{{ang-noun|m|head=hlāford}}

# [[lord]], master of servants or slaves
#* '''c. 990''', ''{{w|Wessex Gospels}}'', [https://archive.org/stream/dahalgangodspelo00thor#page/n21/mode/2up Matthew 6:24]
#*: {{quote|ang|Ne mæġ nān mann twām '''hlāfordum''' þēowian: oþþe hē sōðlīċe ǣnne hataþ and ōðerne lufaþ, oþþe hē biþ ānum ġehīersum and ōðrum unġehīersum. Ne magon ġē Gode þēowian and weoroldwelan.|No one can serve two '''masters'''. They will either hate one and love the other, or obey one and disobey the other. You can't serve God and money.}}
# male head of a household
# [[master]], owner of an animal
#* '''c. 992''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Homilies_of_the_Anglo-Saxon_Church/II "Sermon on the Nativiity of Our Lord"]
#*: {{quote|ang|Sē oxa oncnēow his '''hlāford''', and sē assa his '''hlāfordes''' binne.|The ox knows his '''master''', and the ass his '''master's''' bin.}}

====Usage notes====
* For ''the'' Lord (i.e. God), the proper noun {{m|ang|Dryhten}} is used.

